# PrimeEstates — Deployment Checklist

Run through this before putting the site anywhere public (a real
domain, a hosting provider, etc.) — none of this matters for local
XAMPP use, but all of it matters once real visitors can reach the site.

## 1. Environment config

- [ ] In `php/config.php`, change `ENVIRONMENT` from `"development"` to `"production"`.
      This turns off on-screen PHP error messages (which can leak file
      paths, database structure, etc. to visitors) and stops DB
      connection failures from showing internal error details.
- [ ] Change the database credentials in `config.php` away from
      `root` / empty password — that's a local-XAMPP-only default and
      should never be used on a live server.
- [ ] Move `config.php`'s credentials (and `mailConfig.php`'s Gmail
      App Password) out of the file and into environment variables if
      your host supports it. At minimum, make sure neither file is
      ever committed to a public GitHub repo.

## 2. HTTPS

- [ ] Get an SSL certificate for your domain (many hosts provide free
      ones via Let's Encrypt) and force all traffic to HTTPS.
- [ ] Once on HTTPS, consider adding `session.cookie_secure = 1` to
      your PHP session config so session cookies are never sent over
      plain HTTP.

## 3. File uploads

- [ ] Confirm `uploads/properties/` has write permission for the web
      server user, but is **not** executable — you don't want someone
      uploading a disguised `.php` file that the server would run.
- [ ] Double check `addProperty.php` only accepts image file types
      (extension AND actual content, not just the filename) before
      this goes live — a user-controlled upload folder is a common
      attack surface.

## 4. Email

- [ ] Confirm `mailConfig.php` has real production values, not the
      placeholder `your-email@gmail.com` text.
- [ ] Gmail SMTP works fine at small scale, but if you expect real
      volume, consider a dedicated transactional email service
      (Brevo, SendGrid, etc.) for better deliverability.

## 5. Database

- [ ] Take a backup of your local `primeestates` database (phpMyAdmin
      → Export) before migrating it to the production server.
- [ ] Re-run all the `.sql` files you've been given in order on the
      production database if you're starting fresh instead of migrating.
- [ ] Change the demo/seed admin account's password before going live
      if `seed_demo_properties.sql` was tied to a real login you use elsewhere.

## 6. General hardening

- [ ] Make sure phpMyAdmin isn't publicly reachable on the production
      server (or is protected with its own login/IP restriction) —
      XAMPP's default setup is meant for local use only.
- [ ] Review the rate limits in `rateLimiter.php` — 5 login attempts /
      15 min and 3 registrations / hour are reasonable defaults, but
      adjust if your real usage pattern needs different numbers.
- [ ] Confirm `resetPassword.php` links use your real production
      domain — they're built dynamically from the request host, so
      this should work automatically, but verify once deployed.

## 7. Final smoke test (do this on the live server, not just locally)

- [ ] Register a new account, log in, log out
- [ ] Submit a property, approve it as admin, confirm it appears on the homepage
- [ ] Favorite a property, confirm it shows in the Favorites list
- [ ] Send a contact message, confirm it appears in the admin dashboard
- [ ] Request a password reset, confirm the email arrives and the link works
- [ ] Try 5+ wrong login attempts, confirm you get rate-limited
